    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of opposing reparations often cite the legal principle of the \"statute of limitations,\" arguing that the specific crime of slavery ended over a century ago and that the US government is not a victim who can report back for damages. From a philosophical standpoint, many counter that \"blood is thicker than water\" to argue for individual liability over collective punishment, suggesting that justice should rest on personal actions rather than ancestral status. Furthermore, critics frequently invoke the \"slippage\" problem, contending that granting reparations based on ancestry would inevitably require ruling as either Black or white and excluding anyone outside those categories.",
      "source": "target",
